From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 09:40:19 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-media: keep a session the device reported an error
 for

On VIRTIO_MEDIA_EVT_ERROR the driver closed and freed the session at
once, while user space still had the node open. The next ioctl on that
file used freed memory: on the Raspberry Pi 5 a second 4K HEVC stream
ran DomD's CMA out, the device model reported an error, and DomA's
kernel took a NULL pointer dereference in virtio_media_qbuf() and
panicked.

Mark the session instead and wake a DQBUF or poll() waiting on it. From
then on its ioctls fail with -EIO, and a blocking DQBUF returns -EIO,
but for a VIDIOC_DQEVENT that has an event to take or does not block: it
only reads the file's event queue, so the events still pending can be
taken, while a blocking one with nothing pending would sleep holding the
device's lock for an event that will not come. That is decided under the
device's lock, which every DQEVENT takes, and by the command's number,
so the 32-bit VIDIOC_DQEVENT is let through as well. poll() asked for
EPOLLIN/EPOLLOUT reports EPOLLERR with that readiness, so a client that
acts only on those, as v4l2_codec2 does, dequeues and sees the -EIO;
asked for neither, it reports no EPOLLERR, as vb2 does, rather than
waking a client that polls for events at once. A pending V4L2 event is
reported either way. The session is freed, and CLOSE sent to the device,
when the file is closed, as for any other session.

Change-Id: I0404443d8a94547b693b70b0f3cd254e01d4b85b
Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/driver/session.h
+++ b/driver/session.h
@@ -133,6 +133,9 @@ struct virtio_media_queue_state {
  * virtio_media_queue_state`.
  * @dqbuf_wait: waitqueue for dequeued buffers, if ``VIDIOC_DQBUF`` needs to
  * block or when polling.
+ * @errored: the device sent ``VIRTIO_MEDIA_EVT_ERROR`` for this session. The
+ * session stays allocated until its file is closed; ioctls but a VIDIOC_DQEVENT
+ * that has an event to take, or does not block, fail with -EIO.
  * @list: link into the list of sessions for the device.
  */
 struct virtio_media_session {
@@ -160,6 +163,7 @@ struct virtio_media_session {
 	struct virtio_media_queue_state queues[VIRTIO_MEDIA_LAST_QUEUE + 1];
 	struct mutex queues_lock; /* protects queues array and states */
 	wait_queue_head_t dqbuf_wait;
+	bool errored;
 
 	struct list_head list;
 };
--- a/driver/virtio_media_driver.c
+++ b/driver/virtio_media_driver.c
@@ -661,7 +661,17 @@ process_bufs:
 			v4l2_err(&vv->v4l2_dev,
 				 "received error %d for session %d",
 				 error_evt->errno, error_evt->hdr.session_id);
-			virtio_media_session_close(vv, session);
+			/*
+			 * Do not free the session here: its file is still
+			 * open, and the next ioctl or poll on it would use
+			 * freed memory (a NULL dereference in
+			 * virtio_media_qbuf() took DomA's kernel down). Mark
+			 * it, wake a DQBUF or poll() waiting on it, and let the
+			 * close of the file free it.
+			 */
+			WRITE_ONCE(session->errored, true);
+			wake_up_all(&session->dqbuf_wait);
+			wake_up_all(&session->fh.wait);
 			break;
 
 		/*
@@ -824,6 +834,25 @@ static __poll_t virtio_media_device_poll(struct file *file, poll_table *wait)
 	poll_wait(file, &session->dqbuf_wait, wait);
 	poll_wait(file, &session->fh.wait, wait);
 
+	/*
+	 * An errored session (VIRTIO_MEDIA_EVT_ERROR). Asked for IN/OUT:
+	 * EPOLLERR and that readiness, so that a client that acts only on
+	 * IN/OUT (v4l2_codec2) dequeues and gets the -EIO. Not asked for them:
+	 * no EPOLLERR, as vb2_core_poll() answers, or a client polling only
+	 * for events would be woken at once, for good. A pending V4L2 event
+	 * is reported either way, as below.
+	 */
+	if (READ_ONCE(session->errored)) {
+		__poll_t io = req_events & (EPOLLIN | EPOLLRDNORM |
+					    EPOLLOUT | EPOLLWRNORM);
+
+		if (io)
+			rc = EPOLLERR | io;
+		if (v4l2_event_pending(&session->fh))
+			rc |= EPOLLPRI;
+		return rc;
+	}
+
 	mutex_lock(&session->queues_lock);
 	if (req_events & (EPOLLIN | EPOLLRDNORM)) {
 		/*
--- a/driver/virtio_media_ioctls.c
+++ b/driver/virtio_media_ioctls.c
@@ -1181,10 +1181,13 @@ static int virtio_media_dqbuf(struct file *file, void *fh,
 	 */
 	mutex_unlock(&vv->vlock);
 	ret = wait_event_interruptible(session->dqbuf_wait,
-				       !list_empty(buffer_queue));
+				       !list_empty(buffer_queue) ||
+				       READ_ONCE(session->errored));
 	mutex_lock(&vv->vlock);
 	if (ret)
 		return -EINTR;
+	if (READ_ONCE(session->errored))
+		return -EIO;
 
 	mutex_lock(&session->queues_lock);
 	dqbuf = list_first_entry(buffer_queue, struct virtio_media_buffer,
@@ -1505,6 +1508,24 @@ long virtio_media_device_ioctl(struct file *file, unsigned int cmd,
 
 	mutex_lock(&vv->vlock);
 
+	/*
+	 * The device gave this session up (VIRTIO_MEDIA_EVT_ERROR). DQEVENT
+	 * still works while it cannot block: it only reads this file's event
+	 * queue, and poll() keeps reporting EPOLLPRI until the pending events
+	 * are taken. A blocking one with nothing pending would sleep with
+	 * vv->vlock held for an event that will not come. Checked under
+	 * vv->vlock, which every DQEVENT here takes, so a pending event seen
+	 * here is still there for the dequeue; compared by number, so that
+	 * the 32-bit command (VIDIOC_DQEVENT32) is let through too.
+	 */
+	if (vfh && READ_ONCE(fh_to_session(vfh)->errored) &&
+	    (_IOC_TYPE(cmd) != 'V' ||
+	     _IOC_NR(cmd) != _IOC_NR(VIDIOC_DQEVENT) ||
+	     (!v4l2_event_pending(vfh) && !(file->f_flags & O_NONBLOCK)))) {
+		mutex_unlock(&vv->vlock);
+		return -EIO;
+	}
+
 	/*
 	 * We need to handle a few ioctls manually because their result
 	 * rely on vfd->tvnorms, which is normally updated by the driver
